## Why This Is Asked
Every candidate demonstrates logic reasoning and basic constraint solving relevant to planning validations in software.

## Key Concepts
- Propositional logic and implications (A→B means ¬A ∨ B)
- Satisfiability with small variable sets
- Backtracking with constraint propagation for pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSatisfiable(constraints, numVars = 3) {
  const vars = Array.from({length: numVars}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));

  function backtrack(index, assignment) {
    if (index === numVars) return constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment));

    for (const val of [0, 1]) {
      assignment[vars[index]] = val;
      if (constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment))) {
        if (backtrack(index + 1, assignment)) return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0, {});
}

const constraints = [
  a => a.A === 0 || a.B === 1,
  a => a.B === 0 || a.C === 1,
  a => a.A === 1 || a.C === 1
];
console.log(isSatisfiable(constraints)); // true
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to thousands of variables with CNF clauses?
- What are the trade-offs of backtracking vs. SAT solvers in practice?

