Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about 3-valued constraint problems, including propagation and backtracking, in a realistic policy-like setting.

Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- Constraint propagation and backtracking search
- Minimal use of Unknowns to satisfy all constraints
- Concrete construction to validate satisfiability

Code Example
```javascript
function propagate3Valued(vars) {
  // vars: {Ingest:'T'|'F'|'U', Normalize:'T'|'F'|'U', Train:'T'|'F'|'U', Serve:'T'|'F'|'U'}
  // Simple forward-propagation stub for the three constraints
  // Returns updated map ensuring no explicit F is forced if possible
  // (This is illustrative; real impl would loop until fixed point)
  const v = { ...vars };
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and A is T or B is T, then C must be T
  if ((v.Ingest === 'T' || v.Normalize === 'T') && v.Train !== 'T') v.Train = 'T';
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and C is F, then both A and B must be F
  if (v.Train === 'F' && (v.Ingest !== 'F' || v.Normalize !== 'F')) { v.Ingest = 'F'; v.Normalize = 'F'; }
  // If (D ∨ E) → F and F is F, then D and E must be F
  if (v.Serve === 'F' && (v.Train !== 'F' || v.Ingest !== 'F')) { v.Train = 'F'; v.Ingest = 'F'; }
  return v;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 8 variables with more complex constraints?  
- How would you handle non-monotone constraints or introduce probabilistic uncertainty in truth values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about multi-cycle 3-valued constraint propagation, a common motif in policy engines and configuration management where Unknowns must be carefully propagated to avoid false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F ordering, ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Implication as a→b = ¬a ∨ b.
- Temporal constraints: handling two cycles and cross-time implications.
- Backtracking propagation: prune values that would force a constraint to F.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function litOr(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function litAnd(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return litOr(na, b);
}
```

